    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, I'm trying to refine our reorder logic for some lithium-ion battery cells we get from Shenzhen. We keep hitting stockouts during peak season because our lead times are pretty unpredictable. Can you help me draft a formula or some basic logic for calculating safety stock so we don't run dry while waiting for shipments?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Absolutely! To calculate your safety stock, you can use the following formula:\n\nSafety Stock = (Max Daily Usage x Lead Time Demand) - Average Daily Usage x Lead Time\n\nIn this formula, \n- Max Daily Usage is the maximum number of units you use per day during peak season.\n- Lead Time Demand is the number of units used during the lead time based on Max Daily Usage.\n- Average Daily Usage is the average number of units used per day in a normal, non-peak season.\n\nBy incorporating this formula, you'll hold a buffer of batteries to cover the unexpected demand until the replenishment arrives. It's important to adjust the values according to your historical data and adjust for seasonality as well. Would you like assistance with average calculations or further refinement of the number?",
      "source": "target",
