    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The formula makes sense, but the lead times from our suppliers are the real issue. Sometimes the shipments arrive on time, but other times there's just this random, unexplained delay of 5-10 days that messes up everything. How can I incorporate that kind of variable delay into the logic so we aren't just relying on \"average\" numbers?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Certainly, to account for variable delays, you can create an adaptive safety stock level that changes based on recent trends in lead times. Here's a suggested approach:\n\nUse historical data to calculate the average lead time and standard deviation of your lead times. This will provide a baseline understanding of typical delays and their variability.\n\nNext, apply a multiplier for safety stock that corresponds to the desired confidence level (commonly 95% or 99%). This multiplier ensures that you are accommodating fluctuations in lead times.\n\nFor example, if calculating 95% service level, your safety stock might be 1.65 standard deviations above the average lead time. However, since the delay can be in a range, add an extra percentage (e.g., 10%) to the safety stock to further cushion against unpredictable delays.\n\nSafety Stock = (Average Lead Time x Max Daily Usage) + (Multiplier x Standard Deviation of Lead Time x Max Daily Usage) + (Buffer Percentage x Standard Deviation of Lead Time x Max Daily Usage)\n\nRemember to review and adjust this formula periodically based on the performance of the lead time and any changes in your usage patterns. Would you like to discuss how to calculate each part or to go through your data for this adaptive safety stock calculation?",
      "source": "target",
